# A generic widget taken as a blueprint value in a generic function that calls itself at
# a larger type than its own, `[a]` for `a` (Type System ch. 2: "Recursion at other
# indices needs the signature", so a signed function may call itself at other types, and
# no passage bounds them; ch. 5: a type variable is decided at each call; Semantics
# ch. 8: "a blueprint builds nothing until a block applies it"). The program gives the
# function no finite set of types: each call is at a type one array deeper, and how deep
# the run goes is the number it is given.
# Written by Claude on the claude-lowering branch; subject to change.
emit Ui.*
test mod Tests
use Ui.*

?Pick: fn Bool, @a -> ?@a
fn ?Pick b, @w = if b: ? @w else: nil

@Counted: fn [a], Text -> @unit
fn @Counted xs, label:
    Ui.Label($text=$"{label}={Len xs}")

?Wrapped: fn [a], Nat -> ?@unit
fn ?Wrapped xs, more = match more -? 1:
    ? less: ?Wrapped [[xs]], less
    nil: ?Pick True, (@Counted xs, "n")

@Page: @unit
@Page:
    match ?Wrapped [[7, 8]], 2:
        ? @t: @t
        nil: Ui.Label($text=$"none")
    match ?Wrapped [[7, 8]], 0:
        ? @t: @t
        nil: Ui.Label($text=$"none")
